// EXPECTED: compile error
// expect-error: obtained by dereferencing a pointer
// Gap 41 negative test (2026-04-27, Stage 2): a function that frees a handle via a
// pointer-deref alias (`Handle k = *p; free(k);`).
//
// VACUOUS UNTIL 2026-08-15 — read this before changing the expectation. The header
// used to claim "EXPECTED: zercheck use-after-free error", but the file was passing
// on a LEAK diagnostic ("handle %0 allocated ... but never freed"): the deref-alias
// free was never propagated to the param's frees_param summary, so the analyzer did
// not know sneak_free() frees anything — which is the very gap the test claims to
// cover. The leak error IS a symptom of the hole, so the test looked green while the
// double-free/UAF it documents passed silently.
//
// Now rejected by the deref-identity rule (BUG-785): binding a Handle read out of a
// dereference is refused outright, because the analyzer cannot prove which allocation
// it owns. The teachable restructure is to take the Handle BY VALUE
// (`void sneak_free(Handle(Task) h)`), which the frees_param summary does track.
struct Task { u32 id; }

Pool(Task, 4) tasks;

void sneak_free(*Handle(Task) p) {
    Handle(Task) k = *p;
    tasks.free(k);
}

void main() {
    ?Handle(Task) mh = tasks.alloc();
    Handle(Task) h = mh orelse return;
    sneak_free(&h);
    u32 leak = tasks.get(h).id;  // UAF — h was freed via deref alias
}
